A||||3||Mark||GT Trunk Weatherseal Repair||||1||
Z||000000||Mark||07-29-2000||02:09 PM||||I noticed in a recent post that lots of folks were looking for GT-fastback trunk weatherseals.  Here's a cosmetic fix that worked for me and may work for you.....<P>When I bought my '86 GT about two years ago (with only 33K miles), the portion of the trunk weatherstrip nearest the engine bay was split on the surface which faces inside the trunk.  As a "temporary" cosmetic fix, I applied black vinyl (electrical) tape neatly from one corner to the other, to mend the split.  The tape is nearly invisible and has held up well for over a year.  It's very simple, just remember to make sure your weatherstrip is clean, and DO NOT stretch the tape.<P>I've been looking for a new weatherstrip to "do the job right", but I'm convinced the design of these weatherstrips must be flawed. The trunk weatherstrip on almost every GT I've seen, including an absolutely mint '86 with only 12K miles, has been split in the same place.  I'm not so sure a new weatherstrip would hold up longer than the electrical tape!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||snowkttn||07-29-2000||08:58 PM||||Thanks for the info Mark........mine is doing the same thing, tried different things that didnt work.......buying a new one is pretty expensive.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||loafer87gt||07-30-2000||11:59 AM||||I used RV silicon sealant and glue the split in the weatherseal. It seems to be holding up pretty good so far||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||07-30-2000||10:48 PM||||When I replaced the trunk weather strip, the replacement held up better than the original.  They were pricy little devils several years ago.  But the ripped ones do look awful.  The ruin the look of a spotless engine compartment.||||reg||1
